574:## 17. Why a view, and what it is
576:In **static single assignment** (SSA) form, every assignment defines a new name and every read refers to exactly one name. Where control flow joins and different names reach the join along different edges, a **phi** at the join defines a new name whose value is the name that arrived along the edge actually taken. With SSA, "which assignment does this read see" is a lookup, and "who reads this assignment" (the **def-use chain**) is a list.
578:The IRWB-002 agenda records two decisions that fix the shape of this item (§22). "The primary IR stays a CFG with expression trees; SSA ... are views": `typed.blocks` is not converted, and passes keep editing it through `Tx`, because parity with the book depends on its ids and shapes. And "New facts get new analysis definitions": the existing analyses do not change. The SSA form is therefore an **analysis result**, computed from a revision and cached under its hash like any other fact, with a new id, `typed.ssa`. It is not added to `ANALYSES`: a test asserts that list is exactly the six existing names (`tests/workbench/strategies.test.mjs:111`), and the workbench lists it.
580:## 18. Dominance frontiers
599:- **The virtual entry.** Every local has a definition at function entry (`param:i` or `init:i`). The construction places those definitions in a **virtual entry block** `^entry`, the immediate dominator of the real entry, with one edge `^entry → entry`. If the entry block has predecessors (a loop whose header is the entry), it is then a join like any other, and its phis have an argument for the virtual edge. `dominatorsOf` sets `idom(entry) = entry`; the SSA construction treats the entry's immediate dominator as `^entry` instead.
610:## 19. Placing phis
625:This places phis wherever two definitions *can* meet, whether or not the local is read afterwards. That is **minimal SSA**. A **pruned** variant would place a phi only where the local is live on entry to the block. The view uses minimal SSA, because it is a function of the control-flow graph and the definitions alone and needs no liveness; the measurements report how many of its phis are dead, so a consumer can decide whether pruning is worth it.
627:In Blend, `s` and `i` are set in `bb.0` and `bb.7`, so each gets a phi at `bb.1`. `scale` is set only in `bb.0`, whose frontier is empty: no phi. `t7` is set in `bb.3`, whose frontier is `{bb.1}`: minimal SSA places a phi for `t7` at `bb.1`, although `t7` is always assigned in `bb.3` before it is read and is not live at `bb.1`. That phi is dead; pruned SSA would not place it.
