28:Summary: 'For a new intern: how a pass application is accepted today (revisions and frozen hashes, Tx and provenance, correspondence, the sampled meaning check and its memo, version 2 of the check, the verified rule tables, the mutation measurements), corpus and Candela measurements showing that every simplify and fold edit can be re-derived exactly from its before-tree and the rule data (394 of 394, 838 of 838), and the design that follows: an edit validator, regional obligations through semantic part keys, an evidence-strength order, rule-citing pass variants, a version 3 contract that samples only the residual with version 2, which pipelines adopt it, risks, falsification criteria and a phased plan.'
46:Two measurements made for this guide carry the argument. Every one of the 1,232 edits that `simplify` and `fold` made on the corpus pipelines and on Candela's O2 could be re-derived exactly from its before-tree and the rule tables, by a matcher written independently of the passes (sections 11–12). Re-deriving all 838 such edits on Candela took 22 ms, where the sampled checks of the same three pass applications took 6.2 s (section 14). The same measurements also set the limit of the idea: those edits are 59.8 % of the expression-level edits on the corpus but only 18.6 % of all edits, because most of what `cfg`, `dce`, `licm` and `subst` do is structural.
